  "articleBody": "Swollen baseboards, cracking caulk lines, and peeling paint along the floor are common bathroom headaches. While popping off a piece of trim seems simple, underlying issues often make hiring a finish carpenter the safer move.\n\nHere are the clear signs it is time to bring in a professional:\n- Spongy or discolored drywall: If the wall behind your trim feels soft or reveals dark staining, moisture has seeped past the surface.\n- Unsealed tub and shower transitions: Trim abutting a shower pan or fiberglass enclosure must tie cleanly into the waterproofing substrate without breaching barriers.\n- Loose tiles along the perimeter: If floor tiles are lifting or uneven, installing straight trim requires correcting the substrate or leveling the edge first.\n- Re-occurring gaps and popping nails: Persistent separation usually points to subfloor movement, high ambient humidity, or poor anchoring into framing.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nTrim is decorative and deflective, but it cannot act as your primary waterproof barrier. When our crew at HH Remodeling LLC replaces trim, we inspect the subfloor, address hidden moisture problems, and mechanically fasten profiles into solid framing. If you are dealing with persistent moisture damage or tricky transitions in the greater Denver area, call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to schedule an on-site evaluation.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
